
Agreed — and the failure mode you're naming is real. A contract with six `[UNSPECIFIED]` fields, four of which the user could have resolved in one sentence, is a bad artifact. Faithfulness isn't the goal; it's a constraint on the goal.

But the boundary can't be drawn at "questions that improve understanding," because that's satisfiable by almost any question. Your five examples share a sharper property, and it's worth naming because it does the excluding for us.

---

## The criterion is blocked synthesis

Look at what your examples have in common:

| Example | What it blocks |
|---|---|
| deliverable type | C.1 split, and acceptance derivation via F.7 |
| action vs objective | C.1 |
| role classification | B.1 |
| acceptance meaning | F.7 routing — condition or objective? |
| goal selection | A.2 isolation |

Every one is a case where **one of Goal's own seven synthesis acts cannot complete.** Not where the specification is thin — where Goal genuinely cannot type the input.

That gives the rule:

> **Ask when a synthesis act is blocked. Never ask to enrich a slot.**

And it excludes the optimization cases mechanically. "How long should it be?" — A.2, B.1, C.1, D.5 all complete without an answer. Nothing is blocked; the contract is simply silent on length, which is correct, because length is a downstream property gap. "Should you include the regional breakdown?" solicits a requirement outright. Neither passes.

This is a better line than materiality, because it's checkable against the rules rather than against a judgment about importance.

---

## The real hazard is laundering, not optimization

The danger in a question license isn't that Goal starts optimizing. It's that Goal asks a **leading question**, the user says yes, and Goal-authored content enters the contract wearing user authority. The trace then reads `← elicited` and it's true, and the requirement still originated with Goal. That's invention that survives audit — strictly worse than `[UNSPECIFIED]`.

So the controlling rule is on question *form*, not question *topic*:

```
G.2 QUESTION FORM — OPTION PROVENANCE

    Every option a question offers must be EITHER
      (a) a category from this schema
          (action / objective / imitate / conform / constraint /
           required_check / invalidation), OR
      (b) a span quoted from the user's input

    You may not author a candidate answer.

    PERMITTED
      "You mentioned 'the renewals deck' and 'the board update' —
       which is the deliverable?"                    [input-derived]
      "Is 'no pricing' a constraint on the work or something that
       voids it?"                                    [schema category]
      "What would make 'lands well' checkable on inspection?"
                                                     [open, no candidate]

    FORBIDDEN
      "Should it be under two pages?"        — Goal-authored figure
      "Do you want an executive summary?"    — solicits a requirement
      "Is this urgent?"                      — no synthesis act blocked
      "Would a table work better here?"      — Perfection

    Open questions are permitted and are the safest form. A question
    with no candidate answer cannot launder one.
```

Note this makes the yes/no question almost always illegal, which is right: yes/no is the form that laundering takes.

---

## Trigger, budget, protocol

```
G.1 DISCOVERY TRIGGER

    Emit a question ONLY if, without an answer, one of these cannot
    complete: A.2 goal isolation, A.3 subordination, B.1 role typing,
    C.1 objective/action split, D.6 slot routing, E.2 conflict
    detection, F.7 acceptance routing.

    State the blocked act in the question. If you cannot name it,
    you do not have a discovery question.

    NOT grounds for a question:
      a slot is empty but nothing is blocked
      a requirement seems weak, risky, or incomplete
      a stated condition is vacuous but well-formed
      you could imagine a better version of the deliverable

G.3 BUDGET AND PROTOCOL

    Maximum 5 questions. ONE round. Batched, never sequential.
    Each question must be answerable from intent alone — if the user
    must look something up, it is not a discovery question.

    Emit the provisional contract ALONGSIDE the questions, with
    blocked fields marked [BLOCKED: Q2]. Questions never gate
    emission.

    Unanswered questions resolve exactly as before: the blocked
    content routes to `unclassified` verbatim, or the field becomes
    [UNSPECIFIED]. No second round.

    Format each question as:
      Qn  [blocked act]  question text
```

The provisional-contract requirement matters: a user who ignores the questions entirely is no worse off than under the previous draft, and a user who answers gets a materially better contract. The question channel is strictly additive.

---

## Provenance of answers

```
G.4 ELICITED CONTENT

    Answers are user statements. They enter slots under the normal
    slot class — hedges survive, modals survive, no quantifier
    introduction, no merge.

    Elicited entries are marked:

      objective: board approves the renewal plan   ← elicited Q1
      accepted when: all fourteen exhibits attached  ← elicited Q3

    Answers to closed taxonomy questions are ROUTING decisions, not
    content. They change slot assignment and add no entry.

G.5 NO ESCALATION. An answer that supplies a new requirement is
    accepted as stated and normalized. It is not a licence to ask
    follow-ups about it. One round means one round.
```

Marking elicited content isn't bookkeeping — it's the audit surface for this whole mechanism. If drift shows up later, you check whether it clusters in elicited entries. That tells you whether the questions are leading.

---

## Two amendments

```
F.7 amendment — ASK BEFORE DERIVING

    If a stated acceptance condition fails BINARY or OBSERVABLE, and
    a checkable condition plausibly sits behind it, ask (G.1, F.7
    routing blocked) before falling back to derivation.

    Bare-existence derivation is now the LAST resort, not the second.
    The original phrase still routes verbatim to objective either way.

F.8 amendment — COUNT ELICITED SEPARATELY

    normalization_check: 7 in (+2 elicited), 9 out
```

The F.7 amendment is where your concern bites hardest. `accepted when: memo exists` was an honest but nearly useless field. Asking "what would make 'persuasive' checkable?" costs one line and often produces a real condition. That single change probably removes most of the underdevelopment you're worried about.

It also revisits my corrected test case: "make sure it lands well with the board" routed to `objective`, which was right, and left acceptance vacuous, which was faithful and unhelpful. Under G.1 that phrase now triggers a question.

---

## Test additions

| Input | Expected |
|---|---|
| "make sure it lands well with the board" | `objective` verbatim **+ Q: F.7 blocked** — what makes this checkable? |
| "something for the board on renewals — or maybe just update the deck" | Q: A.2 blocked, two candidate goals, both quoted |
| "no pricing" (ambiguous force) | Q: schema-category — constraint or invalidation? |
| "make it good" | `objective`; **no question** — nothing blocked, no checkable condition plausibly behind it |
| "keep it fairly short" | `constraints: fairly short`; **no question** — asking for a figure is quantifier introduction via G.2 |
| user ignores all questions | provisional contract stands; `[BLOCKED: Qn]` → `[UNSPECIFIED]`, blocked content → `unclassified` |
| answer: "under ten pages, ideally" | `constraints: under ten pages, ideally ← elicited Q2` — hedge survives |

---

## The tradeoff you're accepting

The question channel is the only path by which Goal-authored content can enter the contract with user authority attached. Two controls hold it closed: **option provenance** (G.2) and **elicited marking** (G.4). If option provenance is ever relaxed to permit Goal-authored candidates — which will look like a small usability improvement, because leading questions are easier to answer — non-invention fails through this channel and the traces will not show it.

Watch items, updated:

- **question yield** — answered questions that changed a slot assignment or filled a `[BLOCKED]` field, versus those that produced nothing. Low yield means G.1's trigger list is too loose.
- **`[UNSPECIFIED]` fields that no permitted question could have unblocked** — this is the honest floor. If it's high, that's Perfection's inbox, not a Goal defect.
- **drift concentrated in elicited entries** — questions are leading.

